<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>true</TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- No reference to MeetingTranscriber.App, and not by preference: the Windows App SDK compiles
       a module initializer into it that bootstraps the Windows App Runtime the moment any type
       from it is touched, and outside a packaged host that throws before the first assertion. So
       what a test can hold the application to is its source — which is enough for the thing that
       has to be held here: that no screen carries words of its own.

       The catalogue is a different matter and is referenced. MeetingTranscriber.Presentation is
       plain net10.0 with no references and says in its own project file that it was shaped that
       way so a test could load it, so the words a screen reaches for are real objects here rather
       than a pattern over a file of 157 declarations — which is what a screen's table can be held
       to asking each of them for the right number of values. -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\MeetingTranscriber.Presentation\MeetingTranscriber.Presentation.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
    <Using Include="Shouldly" />
    <Using Include="MeetingTranscriber.Presentation" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.CodeCoverage" />
    <PackageReference Include="Shouldly" />
    <!-- `SignedCms`, which is what reads the signer's certificate out of `AppxSignature.p7x` — the
         one fact in this repository that can tell a package signed by the wrong certificate from
         one signed by the right one.

         The narrowest thing that answers, deliberately, because this project's references are
         decided rather than accumulated: it is the assembly the compiler itself names when
         `SignedCms` is written without it (CS1069, measured). What was not reached for is a signing
         or packaging SDK, which would stand a second MSIX toolchain under `tests/` beside the one
         the packaging build uses, to read one certificate subject.

         It is a real dependency and not only a reference-set one, which was measured rather than
         assumed: `System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs.dll` is **not** in `Microsoft.NETCore.App` —
         the framework carries `System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates` and a row of facades
         beside it, and none of them is this — so the package ships the implementation and it lands
         in this project's `bin`. Excluding the runtime asset was tried and the fact then fails with
         `FileNotFoundException` at the first decode. It is in this project's `bin` and no other's,
         which is where a test that reads a built package belongs.

         No Version: the pin is in Directory.Packages.props, and docs/packages.md carries the row
         saying which area it serves. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
